+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole number of
+seconds, like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes),
+and `s` (seconds), like `1h30m`. Each unit may appear at most once and in that order. Units are case-insensitive, so
+`45S` is the same as `45s`, and spaces are not allowed. Negative and fractional values are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait)
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
